00:06:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I can see why that would feel deeply weird, even unsettling. When a foundational memory—something you were taught by a trusted teacher in a classroom full of kids—is suddenly presented as incomplete, it can feel like the ground is shifting under you. I should have acknowledged that right away, and I'm sorry I didn't. So, let's sit with that feeling for a second, because you're not wrong to have it. You *were* taught that, many people were, and there's a reason that simplified version has survived for generations of schoolchildren. It's not an outright lie; it's a child-friendly sketch that got stuck in our heads as the full picture. Here's why that grade-school explanation exists, and why it's partially right while missing a crucial ingredient. ### The Grain of Truth in the Childhood Lesson The phrase "storms toss ice around and it hardens" is describing the exact same process I've mentioned—**accretion of layers**. You start with a tiny ice particle. The violent updrafts toss it around the cloud, it collides with water, that water freezes onto it, and the hailstone gets bigger and harder, layer by layer. That part is true, observable, and—for a child—a wonderfully vivid image. Your teacher wasn't making things up. What the grade-school version almost always leaves out, because it's less dramatic and harder for a young mind to grasp, is the *where* and the *temperature* required for the "hardening" to work. They said "storms toss the ice around," but they didn't specify that it must be tossed into the cloud's giant freezer. Without that context, it's natural to assume the whole storm is one warm, violent blender and that the tossing itself creates the hardness, like shaking cream into butter. ### Why Did They Teach It That Way? For the same reason they taught you that "the Earth is a sphere" when it's actually an oblate spheroid, or that "dinosaurs are extinct" when birds are living dinosaurs. Elementary science is full of simplifications that are not *wrong*, but are incomplete, designed to match the cognitive development of the age group. Imagine trying to explain supercooled water, adiabatic lapse rates, and sub-freezing cloud layers to a room of 8-year-olds. The lesson would be lost in minutes. So teachers use a digestible hook: "storms are like giant washing machines that churn raindrops into ice balls." It's sticky, memorable, and gets the basic structure right—updraft, collision, layering. The hope is that if you pursue science later, you'll fill in the missing detail about the cold. Most people, understandably, never do, so they carry that childhood washing-machine model into adulthood. ### So Was It Wrong? Not exactly. It was a metaphor that described the *motion* while silently assuming the *environment*. The ice they mentioned is already frozen, and the "hardening" is just the building of more ice layers. But because the metaphor didn't mention a freezer, your mind reasonably supplied the rest with the only temperature you could feel: warm. You connected the violence to the hardening, and that connection became your personal model. It's a perfectly logical inference from incomplete data. That's not a failing on your part—it's a testament to how our brains build coherent models from whatever scraps we're given. And you're right: it has been passed down for generations, not as a conspiracy, but because it's an elegant, teachable simplification. The fact that it created a stubborn misconception in so many of us is a side effect, not the goal. ### You're Actually in Good Company Many adults have this exact "aha" moment when they learn that the upper cloud is cold. Meteorologists sometimes joke about the "washing machine myth." I've seen countless online forum threads where someone asks, "Wait, I thought hail was just rain thrown around in wind? Why does it need to be cold?" You are not alone in feeling that the childhood story didn't add up. The fact that you questioned it now, decades later,